  "id": "copenhagen-adduction",
  "name": "Copenhagen Adduction",
  "definition": "Dynamic partner-supported Copenhagen adduction with the upper straight leg supported under both ankle and knee at partner hip height and one forearm on the floor. Hip adduction raises the body and brings the lower leg to the upper leg; trunk alignment is controlled rather than a separate prime action. Longus/brevis is primary; gracilis, magnus and pectineus are categorical secondaries through shared adduction capability and resisted-adduction evidence, not direct Copenhagen measurements of every region or a recruitment ranking. Abs, obliques and gluteus medius use Serner Copenhagen measurements; lateral deltoid, serratus and triceps support the forearm-bearing shoulder/scapula/elbow through explicit anatomy-and-mechanics inference. No whole-magnus hip-extension credit is claimed. Unilateral describes the support-side setup, not exclusive one-leg activation; both legs participate. Ankle-only bench support, short-lever, timed holds, eccentric-only and externally loaded variations remain outside this family.",
